## 1. Introduction
A wide use of electronic products has brought great convenience to modern life. However, serious electromagnetic radiation pollution is harmful to human health and living environment [1,2].
Therefore, the development of microwave absorbing materials (MAMs) that can effectively resist electromagnetic pollution has attracted widespread attention [3,4]. Generally, the ideal MAMs should be thin, lightweight, broadband and strongly absorbing [5–9].
As a kind of typical magnetic materials, ferroferric oxide \(\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\right)\) has gained tremendous consideration in the microwave absorption field because of its strong magnetic loss, low cost and stable chemical properties [10–15]. However, its large density and bad impedance matching lead to the inferior microwave attenuation performance [16–18].
Inspired by previous investigations, nitrogen-doped cobalt iron alloy@carbon–carbon nanotubes (CoFe alloy@C-CNTs) composites were prepared through a room temperature aging and high-temperature pyrolysis two-step method. The phase structure, degree of graphitization, surface chemical composition, morphology, static magnetic characteristics and electromagnetic parameters of as-prepared composites were elaborately characterized. Results demonstrated that the attained composites presented excellent microwave absorption performance under a thin matching thickness. Moreover, the microwave dissipation capacity of obtained composites was regulated via facilely adjusting the pyrolysis temperature. The microwave absorbing mechanisms of attained composites were also proposed. The results indicated that Co-based MOFs derived CoFe alloy@C-CNTs composites could be promising candidates as lightweight, broadband and ultrathin microwave absorbents.
## 2. Experimental
The fabrication procedures of nitrogen-doped CoFe alloy@C-CNTs composites are described, as displayed in Fig. 1. Firstly, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\) was prepared by a conventional solvothermal route. Then, CoZn-MOFs \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\) precursor was synthesized via a room temperature aging method. Lastly, the us synthesized precursor was transformed into CoFe alloy@C-CNTs composites through a high-temperature pyrolysis process.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structural investigation
X-ray diffraction (XRD) was used to examine the crystalline structure of attained composites. As presented in Fig. 2(a), it can be clearly found that the composition of pyrolysis products changes with the increasement of pyrolysis temperature. As the pyrolysis temperature equals to \(500.0\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , the weak diffraction peaks at \(41.6^{\circ}\) , \(48.4^{\circ}\) and \(70.7^{\circ}\) can be ascribed to the (111), (200) and (220) planes of \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{Z n C}\) (JCPDS No. 29–0524), severally [29,30]. When the temperature increases to \(600.0\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , the intensity of diffraction peaks of \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{Z n C}\) in the S2 is significantly increased, and the diffraction peak of CoFe alloy begins to appear at \(44.2^{\circ}\) . When the pyrolysis temperature reaches \(700.0\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , \(800.0\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(900.0\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , the characteristic peaks of \(44.8^{\circ}\) , \(65.2^{\circ}\) and \(82.4^{\circ}\) belong to the (110), (200) and (211) planes of CoFe alloy (JCPDS No. 44–1433), respectively [40]. Nevertheless, the diffraction peaks of Zn species could not be observed in the samples of S3–S5, which may be derived from the evaporation or existence in an amorphous form of Zn species [30,35]. As displayed in Fig. 2(b), the magnified patterns at the low angle area clearly reveal that the diffraction peak at \(2\theta\:=\:26.2^{\circ}\) corresponding to the (002) crystal plane of graphitized carbon can be observed in the S3–S5 after pyrolysis at higher temperature [38].
Raman spectra was often utilized to evaluate the degree of graphitization [41]. Fig. 2(c) shows the two Raman peaks at around \(1596\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1352\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , which are represented as the G and D bands, separately [42,43]. Normally, the D band indicates the \(\mathbf{sp}^{3}\) defects or disorder, while the G band signifies the \(\mathbf{sp}^{2}\) hybridization [42,43]. Fig. 2(c) clearly manifests that the \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) values for S5, S4, S3, S2 and S1 are 0.93, 0.95, 0.96, 0.97 and 1.01, severally. It can be observed that the \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) values decline with the increasement of pyrolysis temperature. As a result, the obtained composites present an improved graphitization degree by increasing the pyrolysis temperature.
