## 3.2. Morphological analysis
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was used to observe the micromorphology of materials. As illustrated in Fig. 3, after pyrol
ysis, the self-assembly among the leaves forms a unique fourpointed star morphology (labelled by the blue dashed box, Fig. 3 (e)), and the leaf surface becomes rough and some tiny carbon nanotubes (CNTs) are in situ formed (marked by the red dashed circles, Fig. 3(b), (c), (d), (f), (g)). Compared with the precursor (Fig. S2), the leaf size in the attained composites decreases significantly after pyrolysis. It is noteworthy that most of the \( \mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4} \) microspheres loaded on the leaves disappear after the high-temperature pyrolysis treatment. This result indicates that \( \mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4} \) microspheres partic
ipate in the reaction to form other substance (CoFe alloy) with the progress of the reaction.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was employed to detect the fine structure of representative sample of S3. As presented in Fig. 4(a) and (b), lots of CoFe alloy nanoparticles with a mean size of \(21.8\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S3) are uniformly embedded into the leaf-like carbon matrix. Notably, numerous tiny CNTs are in situ generated on the edge of carbon skeleton, which may be originated form the cobalt catalyzing graphitization effect under the high-temperature condition [30,36]. Additionally, the small white area signified by red dashed circles in the Fig. 4(b) should be the porous structure in the carbon matrix. From Fig. 4(c), the high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image shows an interplanar spacing of \(0.20\ \mathrm{nm}\), which indexes to the (110) plane of CoFe alloy [41,45]. The distribution of elements was investigated via energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) mapping. As depicted in Fig. 4(d)–(i), all the elements (Fe, Co, Zn, C, O and N) are almost uniformly distributed on the leaf-like carbon skeleton, which is conducive to the formation of rich heterogeneous interfaces between the carbon matrix and CoFe alloy, thus further enhance the microwave attenuation performance [46].
In order to explore the specific surface area (SSA) and porous structure of nitrogen-doped CoFe alloy@C-CNTs composites, Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) characterization was performed. Nitrogen adsorption–desorption is used to evaluate the SSA and porosity, as shown in Fig. 5. Typical type-IV isotherms are observed, which suggest a mesoporous characteristic [47,48]. As shown in the insets of Fig. 5, the average pore diameters of S1, S2, S3, S4 and S5 are 6.28, 8.32, 5.68, 5.31 and \(6.88\ \mathrm{nm}\), separately, which indicates the mesoporous nature [47,48]. Furthermore, the
BET specific surface area \((S_{\mathrm{BET}})\) of S1, S2, S3, S4 and S5 are 169.47, 176.76, 208.63, 208.22 and \(93.03\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), respectively, which demonstrates that the improvement of pyrolysis temperature leads to the first increase and then decrease in \(S_{\mathrm{BET}}\) of attained composites. This result is in consistent with the previous reports on the SSA of porous magnetic/carbon composites derived from Co-based MOFs [27,49].
## 3.3. Magnetic properties
In general, the magnetic characteristics have a notable influence on the magnetic loss capacity of microwave absorbents [36,40,45]. The hysteresis loops of S1–S5 are showed in Fig. 6. Significantly, all samples present a typical ferromagnetic behavior. Furthermore, the saturation magnetization \((M_{s})\) values of S1, S2, S3, S4 and S5 are 26.8, 28.4, 85.4, 92.3 and \(118.8\ \mathrm{emu/g}\), separately. Obviously, the \(M_{s}\) values gradually enhance with the increasement of pyrolysis temperature, which may be due to the formation of magnetic CoFe alloy with larger crystallinity at higher temperature [40,45]. Besides, the ferromagnetic behavior primarily originates from the CoFe alloy component, which is beneficial to dissipating the electromagnetic energy [36,40,45].
## 3.4. Microwave dissipation characteristics
To examine the effect of pyrolysis temperature on the microwave attenuation performance, the \(R L\sim f\) curves, contour maps and 3D curves of \(R L\) for S1–S5 were plotted. Fig. 7(a1)–(a3) reveal that the \(R L_{\mathrm{min}}\) value of S1 is only \(-7.5\ \mathrm{dB}\) at a thick thickness of \(3.5\ \mathrm{mm}\), indicating the poor attenuation loss performance. As pre
sented in Fig. \(7(\mathsf{b}_{1}){-}(\mathsf{e}_{3})\) , the microwave attenuation performance of S2–S5 is obviously enhanced compared with S1. It can be found that the \(R L_{\mathrm{min}}\) values from S2 to S5 are \(-48.2\) dB \((1.5\,\mathrm{\mm})\) ,
\(-54.5\) dB \(\left(1.4\ \mathrm{mm}\right)\) , \(-15.4\) dB \(\left(1.5\ \mathrm{mm}\right)\) and \(-46.3\) dB \(\left(1.5\ \mathrm{mm}\right)\) , severally. Additionally, the EAB values from S1 to S5 are \(\mathbf{0}\ \mathbf{GHz}\) , \(4.8\ \mathrm{GHz}\) \(\left(1.7\,\mathrm{\mm}\right)\) , \(5.0\ \mathrm{GHz}\) \(\left(1.6\,\mathrm{\mm}\right)\) , \(5.6\ \mathrm{GHz}\) \(\left(1.5\,\mathrm{\mm}\right)\) and
\(4.5\:\mathrm{GHz}\) ( \(1.6\:\mathrm{mm}\) ), separately. Therefore, it can be deduced that the S3 shows the best microwave dissipation performance among the five samples.
Recent investigations revealed that the electromagnetic parameters had notable influences on the microwave absorption properties [50–52]. Generally, the real permittivity \(\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)\) and real permeability \(\left(\upmu^{\prime}\right)\) represent the storage capacity of electric and magnetic field energy, whereas the imaginary permittivity \(\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)\) and imaginary permeability \(\left(\upmu^{\prime\prime}\right)\) indicate the dissipation capacity of electric and magnetic field energy, respectively [50–52]. As presented in Fig. 8(a) and (b), the \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) of S1 and S2 display a slight variation as the frequency increases. Nevertheless, the \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) of S3–S5 show a notable frequency dispersion characteristic, which is helpful for dissipating the microwave energy [53]. The electrical conductivity \(\left(\upsigma\right)\) of as-prepared composites was measured by a four-point probe conductivity meter. The values of \(\upsigma\) for S1, S2, S3, S4 and S5 are 24.1, 41.3, 145.2, 162.0 and \(176.4\;\mathrm{S/m}\) , respectively, indicating an enhancement trend as the pyrolysis temperature increases. Based on the free electron theory, an increase in the \(\upsigma\) will lead to the enhancement of \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) [54]. It can be deduced that as the pyrolysis temperature increases, the \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) enhances due to the increasement of \(\upsigma\) . From Fig. 8(b), the \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) of obtained composites enhances with the increasement of pyrolysis temperature that is consistent with the above speculation. Thus, the enhanced dissipation capacity of electric field energy is observed in the attained composites with the increasement of pyrolysis temperature. Remarkably, the appearance of relaxation peaks in Fig. 8(a) and (b) (denoted by the pink dashed boxes) manifests the characteristic polarization relaxation in the S3–S5 [55]. Fig. 8(c) and (d) illustrate the frequency dependence of complex permeability \(\upmu^{\prime}\) and \(\upmu^{\prime\prime}\) . It can be seen that the \(\upmu^{\prime}\) and \(\upmu^{\prime\prime}\) values of S1–S5 fluctuate between 0.8 and 1.7 and \(-0.2–0.5\) . The dielectric loss characteristic is assessed through the frequency-dependent \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{e}}\) . From Fig. 8(e), the \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{e}}\) values increase in the order of \(\mathrm{S1}<\mathrm{S2}\leq\mathrm{S3}\leq\mathrm{S4}\leq\mathrm{S5}\) , which reveals a strengthened dielectric loss. Meanwhile, the magnetic loss characteristic is evaluated by the frequency-dependent \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{m}}\) . As illustrated in Fig. 8(f), the \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{m}}\) of all specimens exhibits an analogical variation trend as \(\upmu^{\prime\prime}\) with the increasement of frequency. Besides, the S3 shows the largest \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{m}}\) value of 0.39, manifesting the strongest magnetic loss capacity. Fig. 8(e) and (f) clearly show that the \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{m}}\) values are smaller than the \(\tan\delta_{\mathrm{e}}\) values in the range of 2.0–18.0 GHz for S2–S5, indicating that the main contribution of attenuating incident waves is from dielectric loss.
It is well documented that the magnetic loss derives from eddy current loss, exchange resonance and natural resonance at the GHz band [36,39]. The eddy current loss is often described as the following equations [36,39]:
\[
\mu^{\prime\prime}\approx2\pi\mu_{0}(\mu^{\prime})^{2}\sigma d^{2}f/3
\]
\[
C_0=\mu''(\mu')^{-2}f^{-1}
\]
Where \(\sigma\) represents the electrical conductivity and \(\upmu_{\mathrm{~0~}}\) denotes the vacuum permeability. When the magnetic loss primarily comes from eddy current effect, the eddy current coefficient \(\left(C_{0}\right)\) should keep constant as the frequency increases [36,39]. From Fig. 8(g), it can be found that the \(C_{0}\) decreases as the frequency increases. Thus, the eddy current loss must be excluded for magnetic loss. Besides, the significant peaks of \(C_{0}\sim f\) curves in the frequency range of \(3.0{-}6.0~\mathrm{GHz}\) and 14.0–16.0 GHz should be ascribed to the natural resonance and exchange resonance, respectively [36,39].
Normally, excellent microwave absorbing properties of absorbents could be achieved through carefully regulating the impedance matching and attenuation loss [56,57]. On the one hand, the \(|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|\) is proposed to assess the impedance matching characteristic, which is expressed as the following equation [58,59]:
\[
\left|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}}{Z_0}\right|=\left|\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}}{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}}\mathrm{tanh}\left[j\left(\frac{2\pi fd}{c}\right)\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\right]\right|
\]
On the other hand, the attenuation constant \((\alpha)\) is chosen to evaluate the attenuation loss condition, which is described as the following formula [36,39]:
\[
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\times\sqrt{(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime})+\sqrt{(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime})^{2}+(\varepsilon^{\prime}\mu^{\prime\prime}+\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime})^{2}}}
\]
As illustrated in Fig. 8(h), the S3 presents the bigger \(\pmb{\alpha}\) values than other four samples from 2.0 to \(4.0\:\mathrm{GHz}\) , while the S4 shows much bigger \(\pmb{\alpha}\) values than all other samples from 4.0 to \(18.0\ \mathrm{\GHz}\) , indicating the strengthened microwave dissipation capacity. Fig. 8(i) describes the \(|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|\) versus \(f\) curves of S1–S5. Obviously, the \(|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|\) values are far donated from 1.0 for S1 and S2, manifesting the poor impedance matching characteristic. As for S4 and S5, the \(|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|\) values are approximately equal to 1.0, indicating an improved impedance matching condition. Remarkably, the \(|Z_{\mathrm{r}}/Z_{0}|\) value of S3 is equal to 1.0 at around \(16.2\ \mathrm{GHz}\) , which manifests the optimal impedance matching. Therefore, the S3 exhibits the best microwave absorbing performance derived from the optimal impedance matching and moderate attenuation loss.
The underlying electromagnetic attenuation mechanisms of as-fabricated nitrogen-doped CoFe alloy@C-CNTs composites were elaborately analyzed, as shown in Fig. 9. Firstly, the porous structure in the attained composites is beneficial to optimize the impedance matching because the presence of numerous pores will act as medium to decrease the permittivity gap between the air and absorber, which could be expressed by the effective permittivity [60,61]:
\[
\varepsilon_{0}^{MG}=\left[\frac{(\varepsilon_1+2\varepsilon_2)+2\nu(\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2)}{(\varepsilon_1+2\varepsilon_2)-\nu(\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2)}\right]\varepsilon_2
\]
Where \(\varepsilon_{1}\) and \(\varepsilon_{2}\) are the permittivity of air and absorber, and \(\nu\) is the volume fraction of the air, respectively. Secondly, nitrogen atoms are successfully doped into the carbon matrix, which destroys the \(\mathbf{sp}^{2}\) hybridization and then forms the \(\mathbf{sp}^{3}\) defects
and disordered sites. Owing to the electronegativity difference between C and N atoms, numerous \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N}\) electron dipoles will be formed to rotate repeatedly under the alternating electromagnetic field, resulting in dipole polarization and dissipation of incident
waves [42,43,57]. In addition, there are some defects on the porous carbon surface that lead to an unbalanced charge distribution, which is in favor of dipole polarization [42,43]. Secondly, lots of
CoFe alloy nanoparticles are evenly embedded into the carbon matrix that will produce plenty of heterogeneous interfaces [43,44]. As a result, the space charges will gather at the heteroge
neous interfaces, forming capacitor-like structure that generates interfacial polarization loss and attenuates the power of the incident waves by aligning polar bonds or charges under the alternating electromagnetic field [62–64]. Thirdly, electron hopping theory [65,66] reveals that the electrons can absorb the microwave energy and further migrate on the in-situ generated CNTs, carbon matrix and CoFe alloy, inducing the conduction loss and further converting the electromagnetic energy into thermal energy by colliding with the lattice [67,68]. Finally, the ferromagnetic CoFe alloy contributes to a notable magnetic resonance loss (exchange resonance and natural resonance) in the magnetic/carbon composites.
Microwave absorbing performance of as-prepared nitrogendoped CoFe alloy@C-CNTs composites were compared with other Co or Fe-based MOFs derived magnetic/carbon composites reported in the literatures [27,29,36–38,44,45,69–71]. Herein, the \(S R L_{\mathrm{min}}\) is selected to assess the integrated microwave absorbing performance [13]. From Table 1, the S3 displays the biggest \([S R L_{\mathrm{min}}]\) of \(38.9\ \mathrm{dB/mm}\) and wide EAB of \(5.0\ \mathrm{GHz}\) under a very thin thickness of \(1.6\,\mathrm{\mm}\) . It is believed that the attained CoFe alloy@CCNTs composites show a tremendous application potential as ultrathin and high-efficient microwave absorbers.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, nitrogen-doped CoFe alloy@C-CNTs composites with unique four-pointed star morphology were prepared by a facile aging and pyrolysis two-step route. Results of morphology analysis showed that lots of CoFe alloy nanoparticles were homogeneously embedded into the porous carbon matrix, and numerous tiny CNTs were in situ generated on the edge of carbon skeleton in the attained composites. Moreover, the degree of graphitization and static saturation magnetization notably enhanced with the increasement of pyrolysis temperature, which would be beneficial for improving the conduction loss and magnetic loss. Furthermore, the pyrolysis temperature had a notable influence on the microwave absorption performance. Remarkably, the attained composite under the pyrolysis treatment at \(700.0\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) exhibited the best microwave absorption performance. In addition, the underlying electromagnetic attenuation mechanisms were revealed, including the improved impedance matching, enhanced dipole polarization and interfacial polarization, strengthened conduction loss and magnetic resonance loss. Thus, the as-fabricated composites had an attractive prospect as ultrathin and high-performance microwave absorbers.
